{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's defense collapses because it conflates 'risk minimization' with 'rational preference' without explaining why minimizing known errors is a rational guide for future action if one explicitly denies that past success indicates future reliability. If corroboration provides no reason to believe a theory will succeed tomorrow (as the target admits by reducing action to a 'gamble'), then preferring the better-corroborated theory is arbitrary; the target fails to show how Popper can recommend Theory A over Theory B for *action* without implicitly relying on the very inductive assumption (that the future will resemble the past) he claims to reject.","premise":"A rational preference for action requires a belief that the chosen option is more likely to succeed than the alternative, which contradicts the claim that corroboration implies no probability of future success.","premises_essential":["8954631f550790183f505a8637c146b0540564b16cdba0782eb4b486e715f736"],"successor_question":"Can Popper define a non-inductive principle of rational action that distinguishes 'choosing the least refuted theory' from 'arbitrary guessing' without smuggling in an assumption about the uniformity of nature?","target":"8954631f550790183f505a8637c146b0540564b16cdba0782eb4b486e715f736"}]}